 "deeper": [
  {
   "title": "The danger area of the face",
   "html": "<p>The lecture's warning about infection spreading to the cavernous sinus has a name: the danger triangle, running from the corners of the mouth to the bridge of the nose. Infection of the upper lip, the vestibule of the nose or the eyelids can travel along the angular, supraorbital and supratrochlear veins to the ophthalmic veins, which drain straight into the cavernous sinus. The second route is the deep facial vein into the pterygoid plexus, which receives an emissary vein from the cavernous sinus through the foramen ovale. That is why squeezing a spot in this area is not as harmless as it looks.</p><p class='src'>Sources: Radiopaedia, Facial-cavernous anastomoses; Radiopaedia, Pterygoid venous plexus.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why the internal carotid has no branches in the neck",
   "html": "<p>A quick way to tell the two carotids apart in a dissection or an image: the external carotid gives off branches almost immediately, while the internal carotid gives none in the neck at all and heads straight for the carotid canal to supply the brain. If a vessel in the neck is branching, it is the external carotid.</p><p class='src'>Source: AnatomyZone, External carotid artery.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "The pterygoid plexus as a pump",
   "html": "<p>The veins of the pterygoid plexus have valves, and the plexus works as a small pump: contractions of the lateral pterygoid squeeze it and help push venous blood back toward the heart. It also explains why a badly placed dental injection into the plexus can leak blood into the orbit and give the patient a black eye.</p><p class='src'>Sources: Radiopaedia, Pterygoid venous plexus; Brainscape, Veins of head and neck.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why the facial artery is so tortuous",
   "html": "<p>The facial artery takes a winding path across the face rather than a straight one. The slack lets the vessel stretch as the mandible moves and the face changes expression, without the artery being put under tension. Its pulse can be felt where it crosses the lower border of the mandible at the anterior border of the masseter, a standard clinical pulse point.</p><p class='src'>Sources: Kenhub, The external carotid artery and its branches; lecture slides.</p>"
  }
 ],
